//? Title: Zeller's Congruence: Calendar Arithmetic Behind Monthly Data
//? Subtitle: March-based years, exact Gregorian cycles and calendar-normalised airline passengers
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A weekday can be computed from a calendar date without counting through its
preceding days. Zeller's congruence achieves this by moving January and
February to the previous year and encoding month lengths modulo seven.
We derive both the full-year and century forms, account for negative years
with floor division, and prove the 400-year Gregorian repeat. The dataset
application augments `dm::datasets::air_passengers()` with month lengths,
weekdays and weekend counts, then distinguishes monthly passenger totals
from daily averages. An exact cycle count also revisits the old report's
Friday-the-thirteenth question without treating calendar dates as random.
)

\toc

> The data: monthly totals need a calendar denominator

The built-in airline series contains 144 monthly totals from 1949 to 1960,
in thousands of international passengers. Its columns are `year`, `month`
and `passengers`. A monthly total combines demand with the amount of time
in the month: February has fewer days than January even in a leap year.
Calendar normalisation is therefore useful before comparing levels.
It does not by itself remove growth or seasonal demand.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto air = dm::datasets::air_passengers();
  std::print("{}\n", air.head(8).to_md());
  std::print("\n| months | columns |\n| ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {} |\n", air.nrows(), air.ncols());
}
```
