> The method: put the leap day at the end of the year

Let the input be a valid Gregorian date \((y,m,q)\). If \(m<3\), replace
\(m\) by \(m+12\) and set \(Y=y-1\); otherwise set \(Y=y\).
The adjusted year runs from March through February. All variable leap-day
behaviour is now at its end.

Define the month term

$$
M(m)=\left\lfloor\frac{13(m+1)}5\right\rfloor,\qquad 3\le m\le14.
$$
\alttext(The month term is the floor of thirteen times the adjusted month plus one, divided by five.)

For March through February its values are 10, 13, 15, 18, 20, 23, 26,
28, 31, 33, 36 and 39. Consecutive differences are 3 or 2, matching the
lengths of the preceding 31- or 30-day months modulo seven. February's
variable length appears only when the adjusted year advances.

An ordinary year contributes \(365\equiv1\pmod7\). The Gregorian rule
adds leap days in years divisible by four, omits century years and restores
those divisible by 400. Including the month and day terms gives

$$
h=\left(q+M(m)+Y+\left\lfloor\frac Y4\right\rfloor
 -\left\lfloor\frac Y{100}\right\rfloor
 +\left\lfloor\frac Y{400}\right\rfloor\right)\bmod7.
$$
\alttext(The weekday remainder is the day plus the month term plus the shifted year, plus its leap-quarter count, minus the century count, plus the four-century count, modulo seven.)

The convention is \(h=0\) Saturday, 1 Sunday, 2 Monday, through 6 Friday.
The constant implicit in \(M\) agrees with the proleptic Gregorian anchor
1 January year 1, a Monday: the adjusted year is zero and the sum is
\(1+36=37\equiv2\pmod7\). Month differences, year differences and this
anchor establish the formula for every valid date under that calendar.
The implementation validates dates instead of assigning weekdays to
31 April or 29 February 1900.

>> Recovering the century form

Write \(Y=100J+K\), where \(J=\lfloor Y/100\rfloor\) and
\(0\le K<100\). Then the full-year part simplifies modulo seven:

$$
Y+\left\lfloor\frac Y4\right\rfloor
 -\left\lfloor\frac Y{100}\right\rfloor
 +\left\lfloor\frac Y{400}\right\rfloor
=124J+K+\left\lfloor\frac K4\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac J4\right\rfloor
\equiv5J+K+\left\lfloor\frac K4\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac J4\right\rfloor\pmod7.
$$
\alttext(Splitting the shifted year into century and year within century produces a coefficient of 124 on the century. Modulo seven this becomes five, yielding the familiar century form.)

This is the original report's expression. Mathematical floor is essential
for negative years: C++ integer division instead truncates towards zero.
The library now uses explicit floor division and widens the year before
subtracting one in January or February, so even the minimum `int` year is
handled without overflow. Year zero denotes 1 BCE, using astronomical year
numbering. The calculation is proleptic Gregorian: it does not apply a
historical cutover to the Julian calendar.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto air = dm::datasets::air_passengers();
  std::print("{}\n", dm::algorithms::zeller_terms(
      int(air.double_at("year", 0)), int(air.double_at("month", 0)), 1).to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| term | value |
| :--- | ---: |
| day | 1 |
| month term | 36 |
| shifted year | 1948 |
| leap quarters | 487 |
| century correction | -19 |
| four centuries | 4 |
| sum | 2457 |
| weekday code | 0 |
// result_end
\caption(The exact signed congruence terms for the first day of the dataset's first month: January 1949. The final remainder uses the Saturday-first convention.)

> Applying it: month lengths and weekend counts

`gregorian_month_features` reads the dataset's year and month columns and
returns an owned augmented table. The original rows and measurements stay
in order. For month length \(d=7a+r\), every full week contributes two
weekend days; only the final \(r<7\) dates need their weekday checked.
The helper therefore needs at most six remainder checks per record.
A weekend here means Saturday or Sunday; this is not a holiday or airline
working-calendar model.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto air = dm::algorithms::gregorian_month_features(dm::datasets::air_passengers());
  const auto february = air.filter([](const auto& r) { return r.get_double("month") == 2; });
  std::print("{}\n", february.select({"year", "passengers", "calendar_days", "weekdays", "weekend_days", "start_weekday"}).to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| year | passengers | calendar_days | weekdays | weekend_days | start_weekday |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 1949 | 118 | 28 | 20 | 8 | Tuesday |
| 1950 | 126 | 28 | 20 | 8 | Wednesday |
| 1951 | 150 | 28 | 20 | 8 | Thursday |
| 1952 | 180 | 29 | 21 | 8 | Friday |
| 1953 | 196 | 28 | 20 | 8 | Sunday |
| 1954 | 188 | 28 | 20 | 8 | Monday |
| 1955 | 233 | 28 | 20 | 8 | Tuesday |
| 1956 | 277 | 29 | 21 | 8 | Wednesday |
| 1957 | 301 | 28 | 20 | 8 | Friday |
| 1958 | 318 | 28 | 20 | 8 | Saturday |
| 1959 | 342 | 28 | 20 | 8 | Sunday |
| 1960 | 391 | 29 | 21 | 8 | Monday |
// result_end
\caption(All twelve Februaries, including the 29-day months in 1952, 1956 and 1960. Weekdays plus weekend days equal the calendar-day count in every record.)

For a monthly total \(P_t\) and month length \(d_t\), the daily average is

$$
A_t=\frac{P_t}{d_t}.
$$
\alttext(Average passengers per calendar day equal monthly passengers divided by the number of calendar days in that month.)

Its units are thousands per calendar day. It is not a measured daily
series: the monthly totals do not tell us the within-month distribution.
The next block displays both series and totals their yearly calendar
exposure; leap years contribute 366 days.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/zeller_airline_rates.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto air = dm::algorithms::gregorian_month_features(dm::datasets::air_passengers()) |
      dm::mutate("daily", [](const auto& r) { return r.get_double("passengers") / r.get_double("calendar_days"); }) |
      dm::mutate("time", [](const auto& r) { return r.get_double("year") + (r.get_double("month") - 1) / 12; });
  std::print("{}\n", (air | dm::group_by("year") | dm::summarise(
      dm::agg::sum("passengers", "annual_passengers_thousands"), dm::agg::sum("calendar_days", "days"),
      dm::agg::sum("weekend_days", "weekend_days"))).to_md());
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create().line(air.get_column("time").values(), air.get_column("passengers").values(),
      "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2).title("Monthly totals").x_label("year").y_label("thousands per month"));
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create().line(air.get_column("time").values(), air.get_column("daily").values(),
      "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2).title("Calendar-day averages").x_label("year").y_label("thousands per day"));
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100, 460).save_svg("build/zeller_airline_rates.svg");
}
```
