| 1 | 241.75000 | 31.00 | 7.79839 |
| 2 | 235.00000 | 28.25 | 8.30583 |
| 3 | 270.16667 | 31.00 | 8.71505 |
| 4 | 267.08333 | 30.00 | 8.90278 |
| 5 | 271.83333 | 31.00 | 8.76882 |
| 6 | 311.66667 | 30.00 | 10.38889 |
| 7 | 351.33333 | 31.00 | 11.33333 |
| 8 | 351.08333 | 31.00 | 11.32527 |
| 9 | 302.41667 | 30.00 | 10.08056 |
| 10 | 266.58333 | 31.00 | 8.59946 |
| 11 | 232.83333 | 30.00 | 7.76111 |
| 12 | 261.83333 | 31.00 | 8.44624 |

\image(figures/zeller_monthly_profile.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Average month lengths, totals and daily averages, with the daily-average seasonal profile. Mean ratios are computed per record before grouping, rather than dividing one grouped mean by another.)
\alttext(A line plot of mean passengers per calendar day by month, retaining a summer peak after adjusting for month length.)

The table shows a reversal: January's mean monthly total exceeds February's,
but February's mean per-day rate exceeds January's after allowing for its
shorter months. The July and August peak remains large under either view.
Month length is an exposure correction, not an explanation of that seasonality.

> Why the calendar repeats, and which weekday gets the thirteenth

In 400 Gregorian years there are 100 multiples of four, four century
exceptions and one restored leap year, hence 97 leap years. The total is

$$
400\cdot365+97=146097=7\cdot20871.
$$
\alttext(Four hundred ordinary years plus 97 leap days make 146097 days, exactly 20871 weeks.)

The leap pattern repeats and the weekday shift is zero, so every date
recurs with the same weekday after 400 years. Any consecutive 400-year
block contains a complete set of year residues. Counts of a given
month-day are therefore independent of the chosen cycle start.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/zeller_thirteenths.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto cycle = dm::algorithms::zeller_cycle_counts(13, 2000);
  std::print("{}\n", cycle.to_md(6));
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(cycle.get_column("count").values(), cycle.get_column<std::string>("weekday").values(), "months")
      .abline_h(4800.0 / 7, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1)
      .title("Weekday of the thirteenth over all 4800 months")
      .y_label("count in a complete Gregorian cycle").size(1000, 470).save_svg("build/zeller_thirteenths.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| weekday_code | weekday | count | proportion |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | Saturday | 684 | 0.142500 |
| 1 | Sunday | 687 | 0.143125 |
| 2 | Monday | 685 | 0.142708 |
| 3 | Tuesday | 685 | 0.142708 |
| 4 | Wednesday | 687 | 0.143125 |
| 5 | Thursday | 684 | 0.142500 |
| 6 | Friday | 688 | 0.143333 |

\image(figures/zeller_thirteenths.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Exact weekday counts over a complete 400-year cycle, with the uniform-count reference 4800/7. The vertical axis starts at zero so the small difference is not exaggerated.)
\alttext(Seven nearly equal bars counting weekdays of the thirteenth. Friday has 688 occurrences, slightly more than the others.)

The calculation is an enumeration, not a statistical significance test.
A uniformly selected month in a full cycle has a thirteenth on Friday in
688 of 4800 cases. The small imbalance follows from month lengths and leap
years; no sampling argument or superstition is needed. The helper also
supports days 29 to 31, skipping months where that day does not exist.

> Conclusion

March-based years isolate the leap correction, and the floor terms
account for Gregorian century exceptions. Date validation and wide signed
arithmetic make the implementation match that mathematical contract.
Applied to the airline series, the same calendar logic supplies real
month lengths and weekday counts. Daily-average normalisation corrects
exposure but leaves the demand trend and seasonal pattern. The complete
cycle gives an exact Friday-the-thirteenth count and a strong repeatability
check on the weekday calculation.

> References

- Ch. Zeller (1883). *Problema duplex calendarii fundamentale*.
  Bulletin de la Société Mathématique de France 11, 59–61. https://doi.org/10.24033/bsmf.254
- R `datasets::AirPassengers`, monthly airline passenger totals, 1949–1960.
  https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/datasets/html/AirPassengers.html
